\par Though both of them had their own quarters, the Structure Factory was still a favorite place to meet and talk.  All factory storage bays were full in anticipation of the next colony rel
ocation, and it would be shut-down and quiet there, a nice trade-off for the loss of their usual mechanical floor-show.
\par 
\par He parked the scooter next to a service airlock, cycled through, stowed his spacesuit in a locker, and climbed the ladder into the tran-station just outside the factory.
\par 
\par As he approached the big utilitarian doors, he noticed the symbol painted there, the circle divided by a line.  It was a small one, only about a meter across, but still it annoyed him.  }{\i\f5\cf4 Who were these people?}{\f5\cf4 
\par 
\par The interi
or of the factory was dark and eerily quiet.  His footsteps echoed off the metal walls as he climbed the long flights of stairs up to the observation balcony where they were to meet.  When he reached the last landing, Echo was nowhere to be seen, but ther
e was another symbol painted on the front of a supply locker.  }{\i\f5\cf4 How did they get in here?}{\f5\cf4 
\par 
\par "Brook?"
\par 
\par He turned to see Echo climbing another set of stairs on the far side of the balcony.  He was surprised to see that she wasn't alone.  She had two tall men and a stocky woman with her.
\par 
\par "Brook, these are some friends I've been wanting you to meet for a long time.  I hope you don't mind that I've brought them along."  She gestured toward the taller of the two men, thin of face and nose, with an equally thin mustache.  "This is Jacque."  S
he indicated the other man, slightly shorter and at least twice as wide, with a round face, ruddy skin, thick lips and a wide, flat nose.  "This is Gi."  And finally the woman.  "This is Sharon."  Echo smiled at him.  "They're my
 friends, and I hope they'll be your friends too."
\par 
\par Brook nodded without much enthusiasm.  He'd been planning to spend some time alone with Echo, and wasn't in the mood for a party.
\par 
\par "Folks, this is Brook Panati.  I hope he's our latest recruit."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Recruit?}{\f5\cf4   "Echo, what's this all about?  I thought we had a date."
\par 
\par "You have a date, Brook, with destiny.  This is an important day, the day you're invited to join the Masters."
\par 
\par "The }{\i\f5\cf4 what?}{\f5\cf4 "  Actually, he'd heard the name in association with the graffiti, though he'd wondered if it was any more than wild talk.
\par 
\par Echo smiled patiently.  "The Masters, Brook.  We're a group that believes that the reins of power in Eden are in the wrong hands, and that we've been manipulated by the legacy of the Elders for too long.  We want to take Eden back, Brook, to take our dest
iny back, to take our }{\i\f5\cf4 future}{\f5\cf4  back."
\par 
\par It all began to sink in.  "}{\i\f5\cf4 You}{\f5\cf4  destroyed the Gene Bank, and you used }{\i\f5\cf4 me}{\f5\cf4  to get to it."
\par 
\par "It was an accident, Brook.  We intended only to hold the Gene Bank and use it for a political exchange.  We never intended for anyone to get hurt.  That's why we used gas."
\par 
\par "That's why you carried guns," he responded quickly, expressing more anger than he'd intended.
\par 
\par "We're dealing with dangerous people, Brook, and we have to be prepared to return a deadly response if necessary, but we've never set out to kill anyone, even our enemies."
\par 
\par Gi pointed at the symbol on the locker.  "We stand for strength, and unity of purpose.  We wish to create a world of equals."
\par 
\par "Equals," she repeated.  "We have high ideals, and work for a higher purpose.  I don't blame you for being angry.  We did use you to get at the Gene Bank; we did mislead you about the incident.  Not 'we' \emdash  }{\i\f5\cf4 I}{\f5\cf4 
 did those things.  But it was for a good cause, and we meant you no harm.  It's just that you're a useful person, a resourceful person, and we wanted you on our side.  We still do."
\par 
\par He frowned at her.  "How can I join you, after what you've done?"
\par 
\par "The Gene
 Bank was a mistake, Brook, but not a tragedy.  We mean to use the starship to take our ideas to a new and better world, and we don't need the Gene Bank for that.  We're clones, Brook, clones or the children of clones, though we don't think of ourselves t
hat way.  We're hand-chosen, the very best of all the races on Earth.  Why should we dilute that perfection with weaker bloodlines, mongrelize it with random cross-breeding, when we can simply clone ourselves and maintain the perfection for all time?"

\par 
\par She smiled and spread her hands.  "Don't you see, Brook, we're the Masters, all of us, if we only have the will.  And as for joining us, you already have in a way."  Her smile turned coy.  "The Masters have a leader, Brook.  Me."
\par 
\par Brook just stood there, his mouth hanging open, not sure what to say.  }{\i\f5\cf4 Great Maker, what have you gotten yourself into?
